response:
 First find $z^{16}$ as powers of $z$ accumulate by multiplying. Then use the result to find $z^2$. Since we now know $z$, we can find $|z|$. </think>
<u><answer> We can rewrite
\begin{align*}
z^4 &= \frac{64}{5}-\frac{48}{5}i = \frac{32}{5}-\frac{24}{5}i\left(\frac8{i}\right)\\
&= \frac{8}{\sqrt{5}}-\frac{4}{\sqrt{5}}i = 16\left(\frac{2}{4\sqrt{5}}-\frac{1}{4\sqrt{5}}i\right),
\end{align*}
so if $r = |z|$ and $\varphi$ is the argument of $z$, then
\begin{align*}
r^4e^{i4\varphi} &= 16re^{i\varphi/2},\\
r^3e^{i\left(4\varphi -\frac{\varphi}{2}\right)} &= 16e^{i\frac{\pi}{12}},\\
r^3e^{i\frac{9\varphi}{2}} &= 16e^{i\frac{\pi}{12}}.
\end{align*}
Thus
\begin{align*}
r &= 2\sqrt{2}\\
\frac{9\varphi}{2} &= \frac{\pi}{12} + 2\pi k \quad \textrm{or} \quad \frac{9\varphi}{2} = 2\pi - \frac{\pi}{12} + 2\pi k,\\
\varphi = \frac{\pi}{18} + \frac{4\pi}{9} k \quad \textrm{or} \quad \varphi = \frac{4\pi}{9} - \frac{\pi}{18} + \frac{4\pi}{9} k.
\end{align*}
Because $0< \frac{\pi}{9} < \frac{\pi}{2}$ and $0\leq k \leq 3$, the possible values of $\varphi$ are
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\pi}{18}, \frac{17\pi}{18}, \frac{33\pi}{18} = \frac{11\pi}{6} = -\frac{\pi}{6}, \textrm{and} \ \frac{47\pi}{18} = \frac{17\pi}6 = \frac{5\pi}{6}\\
&\left|\text{cis}\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right) - \text{cis}\left(\frac{47\pi}{18}\right) = \frac12\right| &= \frac12,
\end{align*}
the last step of which we may obtain simply by replacing one of the terms with $\text{cis}\left(\frac{17\pi}{18}\right) = -\text{cis}\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)$, and the difference of discussions leading up to it vanish because they end up with quadrupled angles. So either $z = \pm \sqrt{2}\text{cis}\left(\frac{\pi}{18}\right)$ or $z = \pm \sqrt{2}\text{cis}\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)$. Thus $|z| = \boxed{\sqrt{2}}$.</answer>

